From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 22:22:38 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] plugin: a gateway begin whose hooks left the request alone no
 longer echoes the body whole (#5699)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A gateway begin answered with the whole request body on its result
line. Every plugin declaring magpie.hooks made HasGateway true, so
every request went through GatewayBegin; hooks that returned undefined,
threw or timed out — "keeping request" — still had the untouched body
sent back, JSON.stringify on one line. The host reads at most
maxWireLine (32 MiB) a line from the child, and a body over the cap
ended that line in errLineTooLong: the host failed and was killed, and
every plugin on the shared host lost its provider at once. The next
request started a host, sent the same body, and killed it again — the
reporter saw 268 "plugin: host starts" in 2.5 hours, every provider
"connection lost mid-reply (the plugin host sent a line longer than
33554432 bytes)". The cap's premise, that an RPC result is far under
it, holds for every other reply the host writes; the begin's body was
the one line that could legitimately be huge.

An unchanged body is no longer sent at all: gatewayBegin tracks whether
any hook left a different body (a hook sees a structuredClone, so only
a returned result changes anything), and one that doesn't answers with
the run's identity alone — {id, token, unchanged: true}. GatewayBegin
keeps the caller's bytes as the run's body, so a request no hook
touched never puts anything the size of the request on the wire, and
the per-request copy that fed the hooks is the only one made (the
every-request copy of #5168 goes with it).

A hook that really rewrites a body whose line would pass maxWireLine
gives that request's hooks up instead — keeping the request, as a
timed-out hook does — rather than write the host a line it reads as a
broken child and dies on: the wire checks Buffer.byteLength of the
result against WIRE_LINE_MAX, a constant matching Go's, with 64 KiB for
the envelope around the body.

The host and host.js ship in one binary (go:embed), so there is no old
host beside a new Go to agree with; even so the field is additive — a
new host over an old Go gets keeping request for unchanged bodies, an
old host over a new Go answers as before. The errLineTooLong kill
stays: a child that never ends a line is still broken, and the one
legal source of a huge line is gone at its source.

Run (temp HOME, pinned GOCACHE):
- go test -run 'TestGatewayBeginLargeBody|TestGatewayBeginSmallBody'
  -count=1: ok; all four red before the fix — the large-body ones with
  the shared host killed and restarted, the reporter's loop, on a body
  the tests build (32 MiB + 4 KiB) where the report had none
- go test -count=1 -timeout 45m ./internal/gateway: ok (762.9s), zero
  failures
- go test -count=1 ./internal/plugin: the only failure is
  TestHostToldTheGatewayAddress, red the same on the parent commit
  (host_addr_test.go:78); targeted -race on
  TestPluginCheckinAsksAgainAtUntil red the same on the parent too, as
  are TestMothX, TestLaneLimitRoutes and
  TestProxyCheckSaysAProxyPassedBy
- gofmt -l internal/plugin: prints nothing; go vet ./..., GOOS=linux
  and GOOS=windows go vet -tags nogui ./internal/plugin: clean
- go build ./...; GOOS=linux and GOOS=windows go build -tags nogui
  ./...: clean

Broken by hand: `changed` forced true at the top of gatewayBegin turns
TestGatewayBeginLargeBodyUnchangedKeepsHostAlive red with the shared
host restarted; the wire-cap give-up threshold set past
Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER turns
TestGatewayBeginLargeBodyRewriteTooLargeGivesUpThatRequest red the
same way. Restored, both green — each of the two defences holds its
own scenario alone.

